Vegetables, grains, pulses, and herbs (@farming-crops: 0, is #trail, #contains @farming-crop-lifecycle, #contains @farming-leafy-crops, #contains @farming-root-crops, #contains @farming-fruiting-crops, #contains @farming-field-crops, #contains @farming-herbs, #contains @farming-dish-crops) { r_7N1XGTB65EQAAN25KSZPAZMG91
Start with `@farming-crop-lifecycle`. Choose species by local conditions and food use. Space ranges are between plants unless a row distance is given; vigorous cultivars and equipment may require more room.
} r_7N1XGTB65EQAAN25KSZPAZMG91

Shared crop lifecycle (@farming-crop-lifecycle: 0, #part-of @farming-crops, #requires @farming-foundations) { r_5KZF4GS4DZCFWYJEFBNBJYPFRM
1. Select a named cultivar suited to the climate and intended harvest; obtain healthy seed or vegetative stock.
2. Check soil, water supply, family rotation, nursery time, mature spacing, and harvest capacity.
3. Sow or plant at the species depth; keep the establishment zone moist, count emergence, thin, and replace failures only while the window remains suitable.
4. Manage weeds early. Water according to roots and weather; feed according to the soil test. Install supports before roots spread.
5. Observe vegetative growth, flowering, pollination, fruit or storage-organ expansion, and maturity. Use @farming-pests for diagnosis.
6. Harvest by the species signs. Follow @farming-harvest for cleaning, cooling, curing, drying, and storage.
7. Reserve suitable plants for seed or healthy vegetative replacements; follow `@farming-seed`. Remove infected material, compost suitable residues, and cover or replant the bed.

Short-lived food harvests and seed production are different objectives: a harvested lettuce head or carrot root cannot subsequently supply seed. Trial a few plants before scaling.
} r_5KZF4GS4DZCFWYJEFBNBJYPFRM

Leafy crops (@farming-leafy-crops: 0, #part-of @farming-crops, #contains @lactuca-sativa, #contains @cichorium-intybus, #contains @cichorium-endivia, #contains @brassica-oleracea, #contains @brassica-rapa, #contains @brassica-juncea, #contains @beta-vulgaris, #contains @spinacia-oleracea, #contains @tetragonia-tetragonioides, #contains @nasturtium-officinale, #contains @amaranthus-viridis, #contains @sonchus-oleraceus, #contains @xanthosoma-taioba) { r_3GYV35E68P9CCCTG6ZRFTK6C42
Leaf harvest removes photosynthetic area. For repeated picking, retain the growing point and enough healthy leaves. For heads and roots, maintain full growth until the chosen harvest stage.

Also grow @eruca-vesicaria for a fast mild-season leaf crop.
} r_3GYV35E68P9CCCTG6ZRFTK6C42

Lettuce — Lactuca sativa (@lactuca-sativa: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #source @farm-source-lettuce, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-077, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-078, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-079, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-080) { r_2XCEAH6QB8T2STPAAS5MEEWZBB
Cool-season annual; use heat-adapted cultivars in warm sites. Sow shallowly, about 0.3 cm, or transplant young seedlings; space 20–30 cm for heads. Keep the seed zone moist; hot soil can inhibit germination. Maintain even water and check slugs and aphids. Pick outer leaves from roughly 30–45 days after sowing; heads often need 55–90 days. Harvest before bolting and bitterness. Cool promptly. Leave selected open-pollinated plants to flower for seed; otherwise remove the crop and rotate.
} r_2XCEAH6QB8T2STPAAS5MEEWZBB

Chicory and catalonha — Cichorium intybus (@cichorium-intybus: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #source @farm-source-lettuce, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-084, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-085, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-105, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-106) { r_3RW62RCCZHYFCA7CX614B4721Y
Almeirão and catalonha are cultivar groups; do not confuse them with endive. Sow in a mild season, shallowly, then thin to about 20–30 cm. Water evenly and weed while small. Pick usable leaves around 50–80 days after sowing, leaving the crown for regrowth where the cultivar permits. Radicchio requires cultivar-specific heading conditions. Cool harvested leaves. For seed, keep healthy plants through their required flowering conditions; otherwise clear the bed when bolting reduces quality.
} r_3RW62RCCZHYFCA7CX614B4721Y

Endive and escarole — Cichorium endivia (@cichorium-endivia: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #source @farm-source-lettuce) { r_0PJ60CBTMSW3QQ41FVH98R0P5Y
Usually a cool-season leafy crop. Direct-sow shallowly or transplant; allow about 25–30 cm. Prevent drought and crowding. Harvest a full rosette, often 60–90 days after sowing, or take outer leaves. Optional blanching by briefly excluding light makes inner leaves milder but increases rot risk in wet weather; keep foliage dry and inspect. Refrigerate promptly. Reserve separate healthy plants for seed and protect varietal purity; compost sound residues after the food harvest.
} r_0PJ60CBTMSW3QQ41FVH98R0P5Y

Cabbage, kale, broccoli, cauliflower — Brassica oleracea (@brassica-oleracea: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #source @farm-source-cabbage, #source @farm-source-harvest, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-100, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-101, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-115, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-116, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-117, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-118, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-149, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-150, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-151, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-169967) { r_3GHBRDP7C0N2Q88862V4Y3NZNQ
One species, different cultivars and harvest organs. Use season-adapted nursery plants; space kale or broccoli about 40–60 cm, large cabbage or cauliflower 50–70 cm. Keep growth steady; inspect caterpillars and protect young plants with suitable mesh.

From transplanting, cabbage commonly needs 60–100 days: cut firm heads before splitting. Broccoli often needs 50–90 days: cut tight buds before yellow flowers, then collect side shoots if produced. Cauliflower needs an adapted cultivar and steady conditions: cut a compact curd before separation; shade white curds if the cultivar requires it. Kale leaves can be picked from about 50–70 days while retaining the growing tip. Cool all quickly. Seed crops often need overwintering, compatible plants, and isolation; cultivars can cross.
} r_3GHBRDP7C0N2Q88862V4Y3NZNQ

Turnip and Chinese cabbage — Brassica rapa (@brassica-rapa: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #source @farm-source-florida-garden, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-137) { r_705FTDTTPSMM356NMMGKBBSK54
Nabo is usually the turnip group. Brazilian acelga can mean Chinese cabbage, while true chard belongs to @beta-vulgaris; verify the cultivar before planting. Grow in mild weather. Direct-sow turnips about 1 cm deep and thin to 10–15 cm; space heading Chinese cabbage around 30–45 cm. Maintain water and protect against flea beetles and caterpillars. Turnips often reach tender root size in 35–60 days after sowing; Chinese cabbage may need 60–90. Harvest before woodiness or bolting. Biennial seed production and crossing require separate planning.
} r_705FTDTTPSMM356NMMGKBBSK54

Leaf mustard — Brassica juncea (@brassica-juncea: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #source @farm-source-florida-garden) { r_3YCPB8JJKVCF317GJT6YB9R4NW
Confirm species: mustard greens can also be `@brassica-rapa`. Sow shallowly during mild weather; thin to about 15–30 cm. Keep moist and monitor flea beetles and caterpillars. Pick baby leaves in roughly 20–30 days after sowing or larger leaves in 35–60, retaining the growing point for repeated harvest. Hot weather and flowering increase pungency. Cool promptly. Let selected plants form dry pods for seed; catch them before shattering and rotate away from other brassicas.
} r_3YCPB8JJKVCF317GJT6YB9R4NW

Beet and chard — Beta vulgaris (@beta-vulgaris: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #part-of @farming-root-crops, #source @farm-source-harvest, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-097, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-098) { r_0XXZHJGT90YY9Y2ZV594RPDE1P
Cool to mild conditions. Sow seed clusters 1–2 cm deep; thin beet roots to about 8–10 cm, chard to 25–30 cm. Several seedlings may emerge from one cluster. Keep soil evenly moist; excessive nitrogen can favour foliage. Pick tender beet roots around 50–80 days after sowing and cut foliage separately without damaging roots. Chard supplies repeated outer leaves from about 45–60 days. Refrigerate leaves and topped beets. Beet and chard cross readily; seed production generally needs a cold period and multiple isolated parent plants.
} r_0XXZHJGT90YY9Y2ZV594RPDE1P

True spinach — Spinacia oleracea (@spinacia-oleracea: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #source @farm-source-florida-garden, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-168463) { r_4S5X1CSYCPW9D8ZHPHFGZ2P3NH
This is the USDA spinach, distinct from the Brazilian New Zealand spinach food entries. Sow 1–2 cm deep in cool weather; thin to 8–15 cm. Heat and long days promote bolting. Maintain moisture, avoid waterlogged soil, and check leaf miners. Harvest tender leaves around 30–50 days after sowing, before flowering; cool immediately. For seed, keep an isolated population of healthy plants, including the required pollen-producing plants. Clear spent foliage and follow with a crop from another family.
} r_4S5X1CSYCPW9D8ZHPHFGZ2P3NH

New Zealand spinach — Tetragonia tetragonioides (@tetragonia-tetragonioides: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #source @farm-source-panc, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-119, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-120) { r_5N3WMD7Q8WF1320DDE5V06HH45
Warm-season spreading plant used as espinafre in the TACO entries. Soak hard fruits before sowing if the seed supplier advises; sow about 2 cm deep in warm soil or transplant. Allow roughly 50–80 cm. Keep moist while establishing, then mulch and avoid standing water. Begin cutting tender shoot tips around 50–70 days after sowing; retain growing nodes for regrowth. Cook the leaves as appropriate for this vegetable. Save dry mature seed separately; remove old growth when production or plant health declines.
} r_5N3WMD7Q8WF1320DDE5V06HH45

Watercress — Nasturtium officinale (@nasturtium-officinale: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #source @farm-source-watercress, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-075) { r_32PTK6JHA0NCYEDP0VBT4490KJ
Grow from clean seed or cuttings in cool, consistently wet conditions with clean water. A contained irrigated bed is easier to control than a stream. Space rooted starts around 15–20 cm and prevent stagnant, contaminated water. Cut tender shoots above regrowth nodes once established, often within 30–60 days of rooting. Cool promptly. Never harvest from livestock runoff or unknown waterways: parasites and pathogens can contaminate apparently healthy plants. Renew from healthy shoots, clean the system, and contain spread beyond the bed.
} r_32PTK6JHA0NCYEDP0VBT4490KJ

Caruru — Amaranthus viridis (@amaranthus-viridis: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #source @farm-source-panc) { r_2MV4W0G8AE5FMA3AN808ZTDJKN
One edible caruru identity; the common food name does not establish this species. Obtain identified food-crop seed. Sow very shallowly in warm weather and thin to about 20–30 cm. Weed early, then cut tender shoots around 25–45 days after sowing while leaving nodes for regrowth. Avoid stressed or polluted-site plants and follow established cooking practice. Save seed from healthy plants only if identity is certain; harvest before shedding and avoid spreading a volunteer weed population.
} r_2MV4W0G8AE5FMA3AN808ZTDJKN

Serralha — Sonchus oleraceus (@sonchus-oleraceus: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #source @farm-source-panc) { r_6JFS5Y4H6HFZ87873RTCGMWJDA
Use correctly identified edible stock; common names also apply to related plants. Sow shallowly in mild conditions and thin to roughly 20–30 cm. Keep young growth supplied with water. Harvest tender leaves before flower stems toughen, often around 30–60 days after sowing; repeat light cutting while quality remains good. Wash and prepare according to established food use. Collect seed before the wind disperses it, or remove flowering plants to contain spread. Do not gather roadside or chemically treated weeds for food.
} r_6JFS5Y4H6HFZ87873RTCGMWJDA

Taioba — Xanthosoma taioba (@xanthosoma-taioba: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #source @farm-source-taioba, #source @farm-source-panc-identity) { r_70B3P5XM12RNJ0EKT2MGT7F51Z
Use an identified edible taioba clone; older sources may use X. sagittifolium broadly. Leaf shape or a common name alone cannot establish edibility. Plant healthy corm pieces or offsets in warm, moist, well-drained soil, about 0.8–1 m apart. Maintain mulch and water; protect from frost. Begin harvesting mature outer leaves after establishment, roughly 60–90 days from planting, leaving the central growing point. Cook thoroughly using established preparation; never eat raw. Divide healthy offsets to renew and remove diseased stock.
} r_70B3P5XM12RNJ0EKT2MGT7F51Z

Roots, bulbs, tubers, and corms (@farming-root-crops: 0, #part-of @farming-crops, #contains @daucus-carota, #contains @raphanus-sativus, #contains @arracacia-xanthorrhiza, #contains @ipomoea-batatas, #contains @solanum-tuberosum, #contains @manihot-esculenta, #contains @colocasia-esculenta, #contains @dioscorea-alata, #contains @allium-cepa, #contains @allium-sativum, #contains @allium-ampeloprasum, #contains @allium-fistulosum) { r_7VQC7D8F5XQSA6GTFQA7JQ6FCN
Use loose, draining soil and healthy planting material. A nutrient-table common name such as inhame may identify several species; verify the plant before applying a production or cooking method.
} r_7VQC7D8F5XQSA6GTFQA7JQ6FCN

Carrot — Daucus carota (@daucus-carota: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-root-crops, #source @farm-source-harvest, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-109, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-110, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-170394) { r_7YYKDKZ34MHH8DDRTKYKZ337WK
Direct-sow about 0.5–1 cm deep in mild weather into a fine, stone-free bed. Keep the surface moist until emergence; thin to 4–8 cm. Do not transplant the usual root crop. Fresh manure, compaction, and damaged taproots encourage poor-shaped roots. Harvest when shoulders reach cultivar size, commonly 65–100 days after sowing; loosen soil before pulling. Remove tops and refrigerate. Leave selected healthy roots for second-season flowering only when saving seed; isolate from other carrots and wild relatives.
} r_7YYKDKZ34MHH8DDRTKYKZ337WK

Radish — Raphanus sativus (@raphanus-sativus: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-root-crops, #source @farm-source-harvest, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-148) { r_6C3FYXFEVT4JE5F23JR8GVBXSQ
Sow directly about 1 cm deep in cool to mild conditions. Thin small radishes to 3–5 cm; large daikon needs more space. Keep growth rapid with even moisture. Small cultivars often mature 25–40 days after sowing; large winter types take longer. Pull a sample: harvest while crisp, before pithiness, splitting, or bolting. Remove tops and chill. Sow small successive batches. Reserve separate isolated plants for seed pods; remove the spent crop and rotate away from brassicas.
} r_6C3FYXFEVT4JE5F23JR8GVBXSQ

Mandioquinha-salsa — Arracacia xanthorrhiza (@arracacia-xanthorrhiza: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-root-crops, #source @farm-source-arracacha, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-086, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-087) { r_7N0BMFMFA3C46MCTBEK6T7X21E
Batata-baroa prefers mild conditions and a long growing season. Plant healthy crown shoots, often pre-rooted, in a drained bed; typical spacing is 30–40 cm in rows about 70–80 cm apart. Keep the crown correctly oriented and avoid waterlogging. Weed early and maintain moisture during establishment and root expansion. Sample storage roots at cultivar maturity, often 8–12 months after planting; lift carefully and cool promptly because roots deteriorate quickly. Preserve healthy crown material for propagation; do not replant stock with rot or other disease.
} r_7N0BMFMFA3C46MCTBEK6T7X21E

Sweet potato — Ipomoea batatas (@ipomoea-batatas: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-root-crops, #source @farm-source-root-crops, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-088, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-089, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-168484) { r_48S2R0JSZB1402XG3YTZ1B38MG
Plant healthy slips or vine cuttings after frost risk in warm soil. Bury several nodes and leave growing tips above ground; allow 25–40 cm within rows roughly 0.8–1 m apart. Use draining ridges where needed. Weed before vines cover the soil; excess nitrogen encourages vines over storage roots. Trial-dig around 100–150 days after planting and harvest before damaging cold. Lift without cuts, cure under the cultivar's recommended warm humid conditions, then store above chilling temperatures. Renew from clean stock to limit virus accumulation.
} r_48S2R0JSZB1402XG3YTZ1B38MG

Potato — Solanum tuberosum (@solanum-tuberosum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-root-crops, #source @farm-source-potato, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-091, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-092, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-094, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-170440) { r_1MH8S93AD33KT36BQYDKXDN3ZP
Plant certified disease-free seed tubers during a cool, frost-managed growing window. Place sound whole small tubers or properly prepared seed pieces about 8–12 cm deep, 25–35 cm apart, in rows 70–90 cm apart. Hill soil around developing stems so tubers stay covered. Keep moisture steady during tuber formation; monitor blights and leaf pests. Dig new potatoes after tubers reach useful size; storage crops commonly need 90–140 days from planting and mature skins after foliage dies. Cure dry-surfaced sound tubers, store cool and dark, and discard green or bitter tubers. Rotate away from other solanaceous crops.
} r_1MH8S93AD33KT36BQYDKXDN3ZP

Cassava — Manihot esculenta (@manihot-esculenta: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-root-crops, #source @farm-source-cassava, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-121, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-122, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-123, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-124, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-129, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-130, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-131, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-146) { r_7ZGAT9CMCXRABG2EXG1VFDM67S
Choose a known table cultivar for household roots. At reliable warm-season rainfall, plant healthy mature stem cuttings with several nodes, commonly 15–20 cm long, using locally recommended orientation and depth; allow about 0.8–1 m between plants. Weed early and avoid waterlogging. Trial-dig table roots around 8–12 months after planting; industrial cultivars and sites may require longer. Process promptly after lifting. All cassava requires appropriate detoxifying preparation; bitterness does not reliably measure cyanide. Never eat raw roots or leaves. Puba, tucupi, and maniçoba require established processing beyond ordinary boiling of table roots. Preserve healthy stems for replanting.
} r_7ZGAT9CMCXRABG2EXG1VFDM67S

Taro — Colocasia esculenta (@colocasia-esculenta: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-root-crops, #source @farm-source-root-crops) { r_0X9S8ANG90XQH1P593TBPT6YNV
Often called inhame in Brazil; do not confuse with Dioscorea yams or taioba. Plant healthy cormels in warm conditions, about 10–15 cm deep and 40–60 cm apart, with wider row access. Maintain reliable moisture; use a drained upland system unless the cultivar and water system suit flooded culture. Harvest corms when foliage declines, commonly 7–12 months after planting; lift carefully and separate healthy planting cormels. Cook thoroughly: raw tissues contain irritating calcium oxalate. Do not assume every ornamental Colocasia clone is a food cultivar.
} r_0X9S8ANG90XQH1P593TBPT6YNV

Greater yam — Dioscorea alata (@dioscorea-alata: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-root-crops, #source @farm-source-panc) { r_5WZNYVZ2SPTQZB5TRFZ5M5B1AE
One cultivated cará/inhame species; the food names do not prove this identity. Plant healthy whole small seed tubers or prepared setts at the warm wet-season start, usually in mounds with strong climbing support. Allow roughly 0.8–1 m between plants. Keep weeds down early and avoid waterlogging. Harvest carefully when vines yellow and dry, often 8–10 months after planting; cure undamaged tubers before ventilated storage. Cook properly. Retain disease-free material for the next season. Do not substitute unidentified wild yams, whose processing and toxicity differ.
} r_5WZNYVZ2SPTQZB5TRFZ5M5B1AE

Onion — Allium cepa (@allium-cepa: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-root-crops, #source @farm-source-onion, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-107) { r_4VBXHCR87FVF4H13QYVD9GCK22
Choose a cultivar with the correct day-length response; tropical sites generally need short-day cultivars. Start seed in mild weather and transplant shallowly at roughly 10–15 cm spacing, leaving the growing point exposed. Keep weeds out and moisture steady while leaves and bulbs grow. Bulbing needs both sufficient plant size and the cultivar's photoperiod. Harvest when bulbs mature and most tops fall, often 100–180 days after sowing. Cure with good airflow until necks and skins dry; store sound bulbs dry. Seed production is a separate usually biennial, cross-pollinated crop.
} r_4VBXHCR87FVF4H13QYVD9GCK22

Garlic — Allium sativum (@allium-sativum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-root-crops, #source @farm-source-garlic, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-082) { r_17S75XQ5SWD12YT2BWJG46HKXA
Use disease-free planting cloves suited to local chilling and day length; do not assume a supermarket bulb is suitable seed stock. Plant intact cloves point up, about 3–5 cm deep and 10–15 cm apart during the recommended cool-season window. Maintain moisture during growth, reducing irrigation as maturity approaches. Harvest when lower leaves brown but several upper leaves remain green, often 4–8 months after planting. Dig rather than pull, cure shaded with airflow, and store dry. Retain sound bulbs for cloves; renew stock if viruses or rots reduce vigour.
} r_17S75XQ5SWD12YT2BWJG46HKXA

Leek — Allium ampeloprasum (@allium-ampeloprasum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-root-crops, #source @farm-source-harvest, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-083) { r_42D3103SQJ6M6W2NPSMYMRWFXB
Leek cultivars are the cultivated leek group of this species. Raise seedlings in a mild season; transplant about 10–15 cm apart in rows around 30–45 cm apart. Gradually earth up for a longer blanched pseudostem while keeping soil out of leaf folds. Maintain moisture and remove competing weeds. Harvest useful stem thickness, often 100–150 days after transplanting, before flowering reduces quality. Loosen soil, trim, wash, and refrigerate. Leave separate healthy plants for second-season seed; rotate the next food crop away from alliums.
} r_42D3103SQJ6M6W2NPSMYMRWFXB

Bunching onion — Allium fistulosum (@allium-fistulosum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-root-crops, #part-of @farming-herbs, #source @farm-source-herbs) { r_491JWNZ978JWMEB2QRKRXA12XX
A common cebolinha identity; some foods use chives or immature @allium-cepa instead. Sow shallowly or divide clean clumps in mild weather; allow about 10–20 cm. Water steadily and remove weeds. Cut green leaves above the base from roughly 60–90 days after sowing, or harvest whole bunches. Retain healthy growing points for regrowth; divide crowded clumps periodically. Refrigerate cut greens. Let isolated selected plants flower if saving seed, and replace declining or diseased clumps with clean material.
} r_491JWNZ978JWMEB2QRKRXA12XX

Fruiting vegetables (@farming-fruiting-crops: 0, #part-of @farming-crops, #contains @solanum-lycopersicum, #contains @capsicum-annuum, #contains @solanum-melongena, #contains @solanum-aethiopicum, #contains @solanum-paniculatum, #contains @cucumis-sativus, #contains @cucumis-anguria, #contains @cucurbita-pepo, #contains @cucurbita-maxima, #contains @cucurbita-moschata, #contains @cucurbita-maxima-moschata, #contains @sechium-edule, #contains @abelmoschus-esculentus, #contains @citrullus-lanatus, #contains @cucumis-melo) { r_37ZD11R542NHYYWX1ZA4ZM996C
Protect flowering and fruit expansion from drought. Pollination, canopy management, and harvesting frequency differ by species; fruit size alone does not establish eating maturity.
} r_37ZD11R542NHYYWX1ZA4ZM996C

Tomato — Solanum lycopersicum (@solanum-lycopersicum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #source @farm-source-tomato, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-157, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-158, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-160, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-161, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-170457) { r_7MC7038JV1KASC991DXRQX2Y2S
Start seedlings about 5–7 weeks before a suitable warm, frost-free planting window. Transplant into warm soil, commonly 45–60 cm apart with wider row access; install stakes or cages immediately. Determinate and indeterminate cultivars need different pruning. Keep water steady, irrigate soil, and remove badly diseased material. First fruit commonly ripens 60–90 days after transplanting; harvest at breaker stage or full cultivar colour and finish sound breaker fruit indoors. Blossom-end rot often reflects disrupted calcium delivery from uneven water, not simply insufficient soil calcium. Save seed from healthy open-pollinated plants; clear and rotate after decline.
} r_7MC7038JV1KASC991DXRQX2Y2S

Sweet pepper — Capsicum annuum (@capsicum-annuum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #source @farm-source-florida-garden, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-143, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-144, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-145) { r_1WDCDZCFP50Z336R13MGWZ0KMV
Raise warm nursery seedlings and transplant after cold risk, about 40–60 cm apart. Avoid planting root-bound or stressed starts. Keep soil moist but aerated, mulch, and support heavy-fruited plants. Inspect aphids, mites, viruses, and fruit rots. Green fruit commonly reaches useful size 60–90 days after transplanting; fully coloured fruit needs longer. Cut stems instead of tearing branches. Green, yellow, and red nutrient entries can represent cultivar and ripeness differences, not separate species. Collect seed only from mature healthy fruit and isolate varieties where seed purity matters.
} r_1WDCDZCFP50Z336R13MGWZ0KMV

Eggplant — Solanum melongena (@solanum-melongena: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #source @farm-source-florida-garden, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-095, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-096) { r_4EPDSRP5FW3FXZFTSF9J8FBC8A
Start warm nursery seedlings; transplant after frost into warm soil, about 60–90 cm apart. Stake where needed, maintain regular water, and monitor flea beetles, mites, and wilts. Harvest glossy, firm fruit before hard mature seeds develop, often 70–100 days after transplanting. Cut with a short stalk; spiny calyces need careful handling. Repeated picking supports continued production. For seed, leave separate healthy fruit to become fully mature and dull, then extract and dry seed. Rotate away from other solanaceous crops after removing spent plants.
} r_4EPDSRP5FW3FXZFTSF9J8FBC8A

Jiló — Solanum aethiopicum (@solanum-aethiopicum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #source @farm-source-vegetable-catalogue, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-127) { r_2ZB8PEVWRET65X59YFVP42N9HK
Jiló belongs to the Gilo cultivar group; older sources may call it S. gilo. Raise seedlings in warmth and transplant about 0.7–1 m apart in well-drained soil. Maintain water through flowering and picking, and inspect mites and fruit pests. Begin collecting firm green fruit before seed hardening, commonly around 80–110 days after transplanting, then pick regularly. Colour varies by cultivar; ripe orange/red fruit is usually reserved for seed rather than the usual green-food harvest. Save seed from correctly identified healthy stock and rotate away from other solanaceous crops.
} r_2ZB8PEVWRET65X59YFVP42N9HK

Jurubeba — Solanum paniculatum (@solanum-paniculatum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #source @farm-source-panc, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-128) { r_77M9A2PFB3S0RPKQ2AAMR7FF5J
Only use botanically verified edible jurubeba stock; similar Solanum species are not interchangeable. Raise seedlings from mature seed and establish during a warm moist window, allowing roughly 1–2 m for the perennial shrub. Weed early, prune for access after establishment, and monitor fruit pests. Harvest the traditionally used immature fruit clusters once the shrub bears; establishment time varies, so use fruit stage rather than a fixed date. Follow established preparation and portions; do not treat leaves or unidentified fruit as freely edible. Renew from healthy seed or locally recommended propagation material.
} r_77M9A2PFB3S0RPKQ2AAMR7FF5J

Cucumber — Cucumis sativus (@cucumis-sativus: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-142, #source @farm-source-florida-garden, #source @farm-source-cucurbit-flowers) { r_57PZ6P0CB375EQWFVX5FNRTY3P
Direct-sow 1–2 cm deep in warm soil or transplant young undisturbed plugs. Allow 30–60 cm on a trellis or wider space for ground vines. Water evenly and keep fruit off wet soil. Bee-dependent types need flower access; parthenocarpic cultivars have different pollination requirements. Pick tender cultivar-sized fruit before yellowing, often 45–65 days after sowing, and harvest frequently. Chill appropriately without prolonged excessive cold. Leave selected healthy fruit well beyond eating maturity for seed, isolate cultivars, then clear vines and rotate.
} r_57PZ6P0CB375EQWFVX5FNRTY3P

Maxixe — Cucumis anguria (@cucumis-anguria: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #source @farm-source-panc, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-134) { r_5Z3RMAJ0BNBN7BDYSDN6AK0S6Y
Direct-sow about 1–2 cm deep in warm soil with reliable moisture. Allow roughly 0.5–1 m for trailing plants or provide support. Weed early, mulch, and maintain water during flowering and harvest. Preserve bee access. Pick young tender fruits before tough rind and hard seeds, commonly from 50–70 days after sowing; repeated picking matters more than maximum fruit size. Cool and use promptly. Let separate healthy fruit ripen fully for seed and remove spent vines before the following unrelated crop.
} r_5Z3RMAJ0BNBN7BDYSDN6AK0S6Y

Zucchini and some pumpkins — Cucurbita pepo (@cucurbita-pepo: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #source @farm-source-cucurbits, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-070, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-071, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-072) { r_3MZH4EBPBJEYMY7B16Y80MNP2T
Sow 2–3 cm deep in warm frost-free conditions. Bush zucchini usually needs 0.6–1 m; trailing pumpkins need several metres of space. Keep water steady, provide bee access, and check powdery mildew and fruit damage. Pick zucchini tender and small, often from 45–60 days after sowing, checking every day or two. Winter forms need a mature hard rind and cultivar-specific curing. Eating-stage zucchini seed is immature; leave selected fruit to full seed maturity. Many unlike-looking C. pepo cultivars cross, so isolate seed parents.
} r_3MZH4EBPBJEYMY7B16Y80MNP2T

Moranga and winter squash — Cucurbita maxima (@cucurbita-maxima: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-067, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-068, #source @farm-source-vegetable-catalogue, #source @farm-source-cucurbit-flowers) { r_1VMPW1JEZJY40VJ35DWZ334S73
Direct-sow 2–3 cm deep after soil warms, allowing roughly 1–2 m between planting stations and enough room for vines. Maintain water through flowering and fruit filling; avoid excessive nitrogen. Keep bees active. Harvest mature fruit when rind resists a fingernail and the stem changes toward maturity, commonly 90–140 days after sowing. Cut with a sound stalk, handle without drops, and cure suitable cultivars before dry ventilated storage. Keep immature fruit for prompt use. Save mature seed from isolated open-pollinated stock; remove diseased vines from the compost stream.
} r_1VMPW1JEZJY40VJ35DWZ334S73

Menina and neck pumpkin — Cucurbita moschata (@cucurbita-moschata: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #source @farm-source-vegetable-catalogue, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-066, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-069, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-073) { r_5X478T0R3BMA7NV0E4K4RGZ441
Warm-season vine; menina and pescoço commonly belong here, but confirm the cultivar. Sow 2–3 cm deep with several metres of vine space. Maintain water through establishment, pollination, and fruit filling; mulch and monitor foliar diseases. Some cultivars supply tender immature fruit; storage fruit needs a hard rind and fully mature colour, often 100–150 days after sowing. Cut a stalk, cure where appropriate, and store ventilated. Mature seed needs isolated compatible parents. Do not infer the species of an unnamed pumpkin or pumpkin seed from its colour alone.
} r_5X478T0R3BMA7NV0E4K4RGZ441

Cabotiá — Cucurbita maxima × Cucurbita moschata (@cucurbita-maxima-moschata: 0, is #hybrid, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #source @farm-source-cabotia, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-064, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-065) { r_3FCGJQ8DJ3QJQHZHSYD1MS5SD5
An interspecific hybrid, not an additional species. Plant purchased hybrid seed in warm conditions with wide vine spacing. Many commercial cabotiá cultivars need a compatible pollinizer with overlapping male flowering; include the supplier's specified proportion and preserve bee access. Fruit-set products require local professional guidance and label compliance. Maintain water during filling. Harvest hard-rind mature fruit at the cultivar's expected stage, often around 90–120 days after sowing; cure only as recommended. Saved seed will not reliably reproduce the F1 crop. Remove old vines and rotate.
} r_3FCGJQ8DJ3QJQHZHSYD1MS5SD5

Chayote — Sechium edule (@sechium-edule: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #source @farm-source-chayote, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-112, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-113) { r_2NS3JPPYQMAX2ZYE0EQV19N3QP
Plant a healthy sprouted whole fruit in warm, drained soil with the sprout exposed and a strong trellis already installed. Allow several metres per plant; vines become heavy and extensive. Keep moist and mulch. Flowering depends on cultivar and day length, so vigorous leaves alone do not predict harvest. First fruit often appears several months after planting; pick tender fruit before the seed and skin toughen. Retain healthy mature fruits for propagation. In suitable climates plants regrow from the perennial base; prune dead growth and replace diseased vines.
} r_2NS3JPPYQMAX2ZYE0EQV19N3QP

Okra — Abelmoschus esculentus (@abelmoschus-esculentus: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #source @farm-source-vegetable-catalogue, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-147) { r_0N39AARY2E5AY77KCNCQG29KWS
Direct-sow 2–3 cm deep in warm soil after cold risk; thin to 30–50 cm with row access. Hard seed may benefit from supplier-advised soaking. Weed early and irrigate during flowering and picking. Harvest tender pods before fibre develops, often starting 50–70 days after sowing; check daily or every other day. Gloves help with irritating hairs. Leaving oversized pods slows useful production. For seed, reserve separate healthy pods to dry fully; otherwise remove them promptly. Rotate and clear old stalks when production declines.
} r_0N39AARY2E5AY77KCNCQG29KWS

Watermelon — Citrullus lanatus (@citrullus-lanatus: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #source @farm-source-harvest, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-235) { r_5DN8JNMAKQET85C6TDEGFP8R0Z
Sow 2–3 cm deep or transplant young plugs into warm soil; allow roughly 1–2 m between stations and room for vines. Triploid seedless cultivars need suitable diploid pollinizers and bees. Keep water steady during flowering and expansion; avoid waterlogging. Harvest at cultivar maturity, often 70–100 days after sowing: combine a creamy ground spot, drying nearby tendril, and known days from set. A tap sound alone is unreliable. Fruit does not gain sugar after picking. Reserve fully mature seed from appropriate open-pollinated stock; clear vines and rotate.
} r_5DN8JNMAKQET85C6TDEGFP8R0Z

Melon — Cucumis melo (@cucumis-melo: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-fruiting-crops, #source @farm-source-harvest, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-236) { r_6F3WCB7RQC071Y1QCQWV09TGBS
Use a warm dry-enough season for healthy ripening. Sow 1–2 cm deep or transplant young plugs, usually 0.5–1 m apart with vine room. Keep moisture steady through fruit expansion, support trellised fruit, and retain bee access. First harvest commonly falls 65–100 days after sowing. Netted cantaloupes may slip from the stem and develop aroma; yellow and honeydew types need their own skin-colour and maturity signs and may not slip. Harvest sugar must develop on the plant. Save fully mature seed from isolated cultivars, then clear and rotate.
} r_6F3WCB7RQC071Y1QCQWV09TGBS

Grains, pulses, oilseeds, and sugar (@farming-field-crops: 0, #part-of @farming-crops, #contains @oryza-sativa, #contains @zea-mays, #contains @triticum-aestivum, #contains @avena-sativa, #contains @secale-cereale, #contains @phaseolus-vulgaris, #contains @vigna-unguiculata, #contains @vigna-radiata, #contains @pisum-sativum, #contains @cicer-arietinum, #contains @cajanus-cajan, #contains @lens-culinaris, #contains @glycine-max, #contains @lupinus-albus, #contains @arachis-hypogaea, #contains @chenopodium-quinoa, #contains @salvia-hispanica, #contains @sesamum-indicum, #contains @linum-usitatissimum, #contains @helianthus-annuus, #contains @brassica-napus, #contains @saccharum-officinarum) { r_61PAGNRNBNS09S9YZB3VF8WWD1
Edible grain requires harvesting, drying, threshing, cleaning, and sometimes dehulling or detoxifying preparation. Check processing capacity before planting an area whose harvest you cannot handle.
