Rice — Oryza sativa (@oryza-sativa: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-001, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-002, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-003, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-004, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-005, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-006, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-027, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-031, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-169704, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-169757, #source @farm-source-rice, #source @farm-source-rice-water) { r_4QDF9A2QNFP2FX0TM99FEKS069
Choose an upland or irrigated cultivar and its matching water system; rice does not universally require permanent flooding. Sow or transplant in a reliably warm window, using local stand recommendations. Control weeds early and prevent water stress during reproductive development. Track tillering, panicle formation, flowering, and grain filling. Many cultivars mature in roughly 100–150 days from sowing. Harvest when about 80–85% of grains are straw-coloured; measure moisture, thresh, and dry promptly to the storage target. Dehull for brown rice and mill further for white rice. Retain clean viable seed, then manage stubble and rotate where possible.
} r_4QDF9A2QNFP2FX0TM99FEKS069

Maize — Zea mays (@zea-mays: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-corn, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-019, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-022, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-028, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-033, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-042, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-043, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-044, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-045, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-062, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-270, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-169999) { r_7Z9QTMP2V9APEXM583S8XDSQT3
Plant 3–5 cm deep in warm soil, roughly 20–30 cm apart in rows 70–90 cm apart. Use several short adjacent rows for wind pollination. Keep moisture available at tasselling, silking, and kernel filling. Sweet corn often reaches milk stage 65–100 days after sowing: silks brown and kernels release milky liquid; cool immediately. Dry grain and popcorn stay until physiological maturity, then require drying and moisture measurement. Sweet, field, and popcorn cultivars are one species and can cross; isolate by distance or flowering time when quality or seed purity requires it.
} r_7Z9QTMP2V9APEXM583S8XDSQT3

Bread wheat — Triticum aestivum (@triticum-aestivum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-small-grains, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-035) { r_70T0A85CX9HK01JB2AKJNEA9X3
Choose a spring or winter cultivar adapted to the site; winter types need their chilling requirements met. Drill into a firm moist seedbed, commonly 2–4 cm deep, using local target plants per area rather than vegetable-style spacing. Grow through cool conditions and avoid severe heat during flowering and filling. Harvest when heads and grain mature and kernels harden, roughly 100–150 days for many spring types, longer across winter. Thresh, clean, measure moisture, and dry before storage or milling. Reject diseased grain according to testing advice; composting does not make contaminated grain food-safe.
} r_70T0A85CX9HK01JB2AKJNEA9X3

Oat — Avena sativa (@avena-sativa: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-oats, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-007, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-169705) { r_7XDSQRY6DTG8J91T89TKYS3E58
Cool-season cereal. Sow an adapted food cultivar into moist soil, commonly 2–4 cm deep in close rows; calculate seed rate from viable seed and local stand targets. Weed early and avoid excess nitrogen that encourages lodging. Watch panicle emergence and uneven ripening. Grain often matures about 90–130 days after sowing; harvest when most kernels are mature without waiting for damaging shattering. Thresh, dry, clean, and dehull unless a hull-less type is used. Keep food grain separate from chemically treated seed and retain sound untreated stock only when suitable for seed saving.
} r_7XDSQRY6DTG8J91T89TKYS3E58

Rye — Secale cereale (@secale-cereale: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-small-grains, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-032) { r_7B9CZQ46GMD1VRTSB1PX34TY2X
Use a grain cultivar and a cool-season window; winter rye may need vernalization. Sow a close-row stand about 2–4 cm deep at a locally recommended viable-seed rate. Track tillering, stem extension, flowering, and grain filling. Harvest when grain hardens and straw matures, then thresh and dry for storage. Inspect for ergot sclerotia; contaminated grain needs professional assessment and is unsuitable for casual food or feed use. Seed-saving rye is wind cross-pollinated and needs population size and isolation. For a cover crop, terminate before viable seed rather than treating it as a grain harvest.
} r_7B9CZQ46GMD1VRTSB1PX34TY2X

Common bean and green bean — Phaseolus vulgaris (@phaseolus-vulgaris: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-beans, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-162, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-561, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-562, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-565, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-566, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-567, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-568, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-569, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-570, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-571, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-572, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-573, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-574, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-175203, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-173740, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-173735, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-175200) { r_0PC9KK243BFGPV5317KP0PNEPM
Carioca, preto, jalo, rajado, rosinha, roxo, kidney, pinto, white beans, and many vagem cultivars share this species. Sow 3–5 cm deep in warm moist soil; bush types commonly need 10–20 cm spacing, climbing types need supports. Use compatible inoculant where recommended, and avoid handling wet foliage. Pick tender green pods roughly 50–70 days after sowing; dry beans need mature drying pods, often 80–110 days. Dry shelled seed to a measured storage target. Cook dry beans thoroughly; raw or undercooked kidney beans are unsafe. Reserve healthy mature seed and rotate.
} r_0PC9KK243BFGPV5317KP0PNEPM

Cowpea — Vigna unguiculata (@vigna-unguiculata: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-beans, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-563, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-564) { r_0WZDST9W8KQB3TFX8JJWGK3MS1
Fradinho and feijão-de-corda include this warm-season pulse. Sow 3–5 cm deep after soil warms; use bush spacing around 15–25 cm or a trellis for climbing types. Use suitable inoculant where advised and control weeds before canopy closure. Heat tolerance exceeds that of many common beans, but flowering and filling still need moisture. Harvest green pods or fresh seed at the cultivar's eating stage; dry grain often needs 70–100 days after sowing. Dry and protect against storage insects. Save healthy mature seed; cultivar and flowering time determine isolation needs.
} r_0WZDST9W8KQB3TFX8JJWGK3MS1

Mung bean — Vigna radiata (@vigna-radiata: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-mungbean) { r_2Z61S286ETYKQ53QVWFKF8ASB1
A common source of broto de feijão, but the food name alone cannot exclude soybean or other sprouts. Sow 2–4 cm deep in warm drained soil, roughly 10–15 cm apart in rows. Keep weeds down early and moisture available during flowering. Harvest dry pods as they mature, often from 60–90 days after sowing, before shattering. Dry and clean grain for storage. Sprouting is a separate controlled food-production process: use seed sold for edible sprouting, never treated field seed. Warm wet sprouting conditions can amplify pathogens; garden-clean appearance does not demonstrate safety.
} r_2Z61S286ETYKQ53QVWFKF8ASB1

Pea — Pisum sativum (@pisum-sativum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-peas, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-559, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-560, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-170420) { r_6HFFA6X2S010TGA7FBFCAN46FD
Sow directly 3–5 cm deep during a cool growing window, about 5–8 cm apart; support tall types. Avoid waterlogged soil and heat during flowering. Use compatible inoculant when appropriate. Shelling peas are ready when pods fill but seeds remain sweet and tender; snow peas are picked flat and snap peas plump before toughness. First picking commonly occurs 55–80 days after sowing. Pick frequently and cool immediately. For dry grain or seed, leave healthy pods to mature, dry, shell, and store sound seed; follow with a different crop family.
} r_6HFFA6X2S010TGA7FBFCAN46FD

Chickpea — Cicer arietinum (@cicer-arietinum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-pulses, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-575, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-173757) { r_1C30MZCVTF22MA1Y5WFZKQPH4M
Choose a locally suitable cool-to-mild season with dry harvest weather. Sow about 3–5 cm deep, 10–15 cm apart in rows 30–50 cm apart; use compatible inoculant if advised. Avoid waterlogging and prolonged wet foliage; monitor pod diseases. Harvest green pods for fresh seed or let the crop dry for mature grain, commonly 90–130 days after sowing. Thresh gently, clean, measure moisture, and store dry. Keep healthy mature untreated seed for propagation; rotate and avoid following another crop carrying the same root disease.
} r_1C30MZCVTF22MA1Y5WFZKQPH4M

Pigeon pea — Cajanus cajan (@cajanus-cajan: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-pigeonpea, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-576) { r_064FQHT70C1KWQM789M08SB80J
Guandu is a warm-season shrub pulse. Sow 2–4 cm deep at reliable warm-season moisture; allow roughly 0.5–1 m or more for perennial forms. Inoculate if locally recommended, weed early, and prune only to the chosen grain or hedgerow system. Short-duration cultivars may supply pods within 4–6 months; long-duration types respond strongly to day length and take longer. Pick green seed or dry mature pods; dry grain before storage. Retain healthy seed and prune or renew declining plants. Do not promise nitrogen benefits without accounting for harvested biomass.
} r_064FQHT70C1KWQM789M08SB80J

Lentil — Lens culinaris (@lens-culinaris: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-pulses, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-577, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-578, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-172421) { r_29GDB66Y6NZBZP4YY6T40N39M2
Cool-season pulse that needs a suitable dry finishing period. Sow about 2–4 cm deep in close rows, with locally recommended seed density and compatible inoculant where needed. Weed early; small plants compete poorly. Avoid saturated soil and dense humid canopies. Harvest when lower pods mature and seeds are firm, often 90–120 days after sowing; waiting for every pod to dry increases shattering losses. Thresh, clean, dry, and store grain at a measured safe moisture. Save healthy untreated seed and rotate to reduce persistent root diseases.
} r_29GDB66Y6NZBZP4YY6T40N39M2

Soybean and edamame — Glycine max (@glycine-max: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-soybean, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-272, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-581, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-584, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-168411, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-171411) { r_6W15X0A628P9SJETFXN1BJHQ4S
Choose a cultivar whose maturity group suits local day length; edamame needs a food-quality vegetable cultivar. Sow roughly 3–5 cm deep in warm drained soil and use compatible Bradyrhizobium inoculant where needed. Set stand density from local recommendations. Protect flowering and pod fill from drought. Pick edamame when pods are green and fully filled, often 70–100 days after sowing; cook before eating. Dry soybeans stay until mature and need measured drying, cleaning, and food processing. Flour, oil, and tofu share the crop identity but not nutrient composition. Retain healthy seed only when suitable and rotate.
} r_6W15X0A628P9SJETFXN1BJHQ4S

White lupin — Lupinus albus (@lupinus-albus: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-lupin) { r_6BWXQR5S4WBTHBD5KQCWW9K4XK
One possible tremoço species; the food record does not distinguish L. albus from other cultivated lupins. Select a certified food cultivar adapted to mild conditions and appropriate soil. Sow about 3–5 cm deep with species-specific inoculation and local stand density. Avoid waterlogging. Harvest dry mature pods before shattering, thresh, and dry. Bitter lupins contain toxic alkaloids; a common name, home taste test, or one soaking cycle cannot establish safety. Use verified low-alkaloid food stock and validated preparation. Keep cultivar identity through seed saving and never substitute ornamental lupins.
} r_6BWXQR5S4WBTHBD5KQCWW9K4XK

Peanut — Arachis hypogaea (@arachis-hypogaea: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-peanut, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-557, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-173806) { r_4BJJ52DQMGAXDY77C3ME0APVCC
Sow sound untreated seed about 4–6 cm deep in warm, loose soil, roughly 10–20 cm apart with row access. Use compatible inoculant where needed. Flowers form above ground; fertilized pegs enter soil to develop pods. Avoid deep cultivation after pegging and maintain moisture during filling. Trial-dig around 110–150 days after sowing and judge pod maturity by cultivar methods, not foliage alone. Lift carefully and dry promptly; warm wet storage encourages aflatoxin-producing moulds. Mouldy nuts cannot be made safe by ordinary roasting. Save only sound mature untreated seed and rotate.
} r_4BJJ52DQMGAXDY77C3ME0APVCC

Quinoa — Chenopodium quinoa (@chenopodium-quinoa: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-quinoa, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-168917) { r_7HVG1A299WEJ1X6JSFZA3KXYNH
Choose a cultivar adapted to elevation, heat, and day length. Sow shallowly, around 0.5–1 cm, into a moist fine bed; thin to about 15–30 cm with row access. Weed early and avoid waterlogging and severe heat at flowering. Harvest mature seed heads as seed hardens and plants dry, commonly 90–150 days after sowing, before rain or shattering losses. Dry, thresh, and clean. Remove bitter surface saponins using an established food-processing method appropriate to the cultivar. Retain viable clean seed and contain volunteers.
} r_7HVG1A299WEJ1X6JSFZA3KXYNH

Chia — Salvia hispanica (@salvia-hispanica: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-chia, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-170554) { r_2266RYG64V5SJ4WGYDAQVQJFV0
Warm-season annual. Many traditional cultivars flower under short days; a long frost-free period alone may not permit seed harvest at high latitude. Choose an adapted cultivar, sow tiny seed shallowly into a moist seedbed, and thin to roughly 20–40 cm. Control weeds early and maintain drainage. Harvest when flower heads dry and mature seed separates readily, before shattering; the cycle often spans several months and depends strongly on flowering response. Dry, thresh, clean, and store sound seed. Reserve a labelled mature seed lot and rotate.
} r_2266RYG64V5SJ4WGYDAQVQJFV0

Sesame — Sesamum indicum (@sesamum-indicum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-sesame, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-593, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-170150) { r_70G1YA5BZXGBZVXXE2A2A1X6GE
Sow shallowly, about 1–2 cm, into reliably warm moist soil; thin to 10–20 cm with row access. Small seedlings need early weed control. Established plants tolerate some drought but are sensitive to waterlogging; flowering and seed filling benefit from water. Harvest when lower capsules mature, commonly 90–130 days after sowing; shattering types must be cut before all pods open. Dry upright over a clean collection surface, then thresh and clean. Choose non-shattering cultivars where appropriate. Store dry and save healthy mature seed.
} r_70G1YA5BZXGBZVXXE2A2A1X6GE

Flax — Linum usitatissimum (@linum-usitatissimum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-flax, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-594, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-169414) { r_17G79C6QAF9RD7CSJBJGXXCM0X
Choose a food oilseed cultivar rather than assuming a fibre cultivar gives the same harvest. Sow a close-row stand about 2–3 cm deep in a cool-to-mild growing window using local density guidance. Weed early; avoid excess nitrogen and lodging. Harvest when most capsules brown and seed rattles, commonly 90–120 days after sowing. Dry plants if necessary, thresh, clean, measure moisture, and protect oil-rich seed from heat and damp storage. Keep viable untreated seed separately and rotate to reduce disease carryover.
} r_17G79C6QAF9RD7CSJBJGXXCM0X

Sunflower — Helianthus annuus (@helianthus-annuus: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-sunflower, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-269, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-170562) { r_5W727R44CTG4Q4DYVNWXMZSNQV
Select an edible-seed or oil cultivar. Sow 2–4 cm deep after severe cold risk, usually 20–45 cm apart according to head size. Keep weeds down early and water during flowering and seed filling. Support tall plants where wind threatens. Harvest when the back of the head yellows to brown and seeds harden, often 90–120 days after sowing. Protect ripening heads from birds without trapping wildlife. Dry heads with airflow, thresh, and store dry; discard mouldy seed. Isolate seed parents from other flowering sunflowers when retaining cultivar traits.
} r_5W727R44CTG4Q4DYVNWXMZSNQV

Canola — Brassica napus (@brassica-napus: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-canola, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-268, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-172336) { r_3GZQSAY6405MKPEFEB19TC4WRG
Canola is a quality class of rapeseed; commercial oil can also come from other Brassica species. Use a verified low-erucic-acid, low-glucosinolate canola cultivar suited to the season. Sow shallowly, about 1–2 cm, into firm moist soil at local stand density. Monitor flea beetles and flowering pests. Harvest by pod and seed colour change before shattering; spring types commonly need 90–140 days from sowing. Dry, clean, and use suitable oil-processing equipment. Do not substitute ornamental or unidentified rapeseed. Retain seed only if the cultivar's genetics and health make this appropriate.
} r_3GZQSAY6405MKPEFEB19TC4WRG

Sugarcane — Saccharum officinarum (@saccharum-officinarum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-field-crops, #source @farm-source-sugarcane, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-492, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-493, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-494, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-508, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-510) { r_3RYQWB675AZ98ECR6Q6VG2KGEF
Modern sugarcane is commonly an interspecific Saccharum hybrid complex; this conventional species heading is an origin reference, not proof of the table sugar cultivar. Plant healthy budded stalk setts in warm moist furrows at locally recommended spacing. Weed early, feed from soil tests, and maintain water during growth. Harvest mature high-sugar stalks using cultivar and juice-maturity criteria, commonly 12–18 months for plant cane. Crush promptly with guarded equipment; sugar, molasses, and rapadura require controlled food processing. Manage healthy ratoon regrowth or replant when yield or health declines; avoid burning residues as a routine.
} r_3RYQWB675AZ98ECR6Q6VG2KGEF

Culinary herbs and aromatic leaves (@farming-herbs: 0, #part-of @farming-crops, #contains @ocimum-basilicum, #contains @ocimum-gratissimum, #contains @coriandrum-sativum, #contains @petroselinum-crispum, #contains @apium-graveolens, #contains @foeniculum-vulgare) { r_00SS8A2ZMRYXBNJKRTVKZZW1T4
