Sweet basil — Ocimum basilicum (@ocimum-basilicum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-herbs, #source @farm-source-herbs, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-133) { r_79P40CT6KZD8MGFV4BS79HWZ9V
Manjericão commonly means this species. Sow shallowly or root healthy cuttings in warm conditions; transplant around 25–40 cm apart after cold risk. Water soil rather than keeping leaves wet. Pinch tips above a node to encourage branches and remove flower spikes when leaf production is the aim. Start light picking roughly 40–60 days after sowing. Use promptly; basil is sensitive to chilling. Leave selected plants to form mature seed if required, then renew declining plants from healthy seed or cuttings.
} r_79P40CT6KZD8MGFV4BS79HWZ9V

Clove basil — Ocimum gratissimum (@ocimum-gratissimum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-herbs, #source @farm-source-alfavaca) { r_5YC7A8Z4PRG5CD30MVJ4ES1FF6
One alfavaca identity; the nutrition name does not establish which Ocimum species was measured. Plant identified seedling or rooted cutting in warm drained soil, allowing roughly 0.6–1 m for the perennial shrub. Establish with regular water, then prune lightly to keep leafy branches accessible. Pick healthy mature leaves after establishment and before heavy flowering when the aim is aromatic foliage. Remove dead or diseased branches and renew from healthy cuttings or seed. Do not transfer culinary use of a named basil to concentrated essential oil preparations.
} r_5YC7A8Z4PRG5CD30MVJ4ES1FF6

Coriander — Coriandrum sativum (@coriandrum-sativum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-herbs, #source @farm-source-herbs, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-114) { r_3E1B7X3H70YNB5BGSH67SP3Y9T
Direct-sow about 1 cm deep in mild weather; thin leafy crops to roughly 10–15 cm. Heat and long days accelerate bolting. Keep the seedbed moist and sow small successive batches. Cut leaves around 30–50 days after sowing, retaining some growing tissue, or pull whole bunches. Seed spice needs flowering and fully brown dry fruits, often 90–120 days from sowing; collect before shedding. Dry and store leaves or seed by appropriate methods. Retain sound mature seed and rotate rather than repeatedly sowing into an exhausted unweeded patch.
} r_3E1B7X3H70YNB5BGSH67SP3Y9T

Parsley — Petroselinum crispum (@petroselinum-crispum: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-herbs, #source @farm-source-herbs, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-153) { r_72P8AKNHRVMNT7ZJS8TGVZN5TA
Sow about 0.5–1 cm deep in mild conditions; germination can take several weeks, so maintain gentle moisture and label the bed. Transplant young seedlings if preferred and allow 15–25 cm. Harvest outer stalks from roughly 70–100 days after sowing, leaving central growth. Water evenly, check leaf pests, and cool harvested leaves. It is usually biennial: second-season flowering reduces leaf quality but supplies seed after suitable winter exposure. Keep seed plants separate from the main leaf-harvest succession and replace declining plants.
} r_72P8AKNHRVMNT7ZJS8TGVZN5TA

Celery — Apium graveolens (@apium-graveolens: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-herbs, #source @farm-source-celery, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-076) { r_6QQRX16JZZT6K5QM61NGYZ31WE
Aipo cultivars differ between stalk, leaf, and root uses. Surface-sow or cover tiny seed minimally in a controlled nursery; transplant during a mild season about 20–30 cm apart. Avoid burying the growing point. Celery needs steady moisture and a long uninterrupted growth period; stress and unsuitable cold exposure can cause bolting. Pick usable outer stalks or whole plants, commonly 90–130 days after transplanting for stalk types. Cool promptly. Keep separate plants through their flowering cycle for seed, or clear and replant for continued leaf production.
} r_6QQRX16JZZT6K5QM61NGYZ31WE

Fennel — Foeniculum vulgare (@foeniculum-vulgare: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-herbs, #source @farm-source-herbs) { r_2G7F1JP683BS9HNB28CZTD4RF4
One erva-doce identity; anise tea can instead use Pimpinella anisum, so confirm the supplier. Sow about 1 cm deep in mild weather and thin to around 25–40 cm for Florence fennel; seed-spice forms may need more space. Keep water even. Pick young leaves as needed, swollen leaf bases before bolting, or dry brown fruits for spice after flowering. Harvest timing depends strongly on the chosen use and cultivar. Collect seed before shedding and contain volunteers; remove exhausted annual crops or prune perennial forms for healthy renewal.
} r_2G7F1JP683BS9HNB28CZTD4RF4
Rocket — Eruca vesicaria (@eruca-vesicaria: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-leafy-crops, #source @farm-source-florida-garden, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-152) { r_5JH2QT3CH9BG4ZPJ8PAMJR4MB4
Cultivated rúcula is commonly the sativa subspecies; wild rocket can be Diplotaxis instead. Sow about 0.5–1 cm deep during mild weather and thin to 10–15 cm for larger leaves. Keep moisture steady and protect young foliage against flea beetles. Pick tender leaves roughly 25–40 days after sowing, retaining growing points for repeated cutting; harvest before coarse flowering stems dominate. Cool promptly and sow successive small batches. Let isolated healthy plants mature dry pods for seed, or remove them before shedding and follow with a non-brassica crop.
} r_5JH2QT3CH9BG4ZPJ8PAMJR4MB4

